  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $5.6B on contracts in FY2027 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs recorded 11,569 awards with an average award size of $487K. The Department of Veterans Affairs's obligations are down 93.4% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year moved from $56.4B in FY22, $61.7B in FY23, $66.9B in FY24, $78.3B in FY25, and $85.8B in FY26 to $5.6B in FY27. The Top 10 NAICS categories in FY2027 to date are led by Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $1.1B, followed by Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) at $457M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $411M. The Top 10 vendors in FY2027 to date are led by TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $900M, then QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $247M and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC at $226M. The Department of Veterans Affairs's current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the sharp year-over-year decline in FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs's FY2027 to date obligations are much lower than prior fiscal years, falling from $85.8B in FY26 to $5.6B in FY27. The Department of Veterans Affairs's award count is 11,569 with an average award size of $487K, which reflects current-year, fiscal year to date totals rather than a full-year result. The Department of Veterans Affairs's reported change is down 93.4% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is the Department of Veterans Affairs spending its FY2027 to date contract dollars?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is concentrating FY2027 to date obligations in health and medical services and construction: Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers received $1.1B and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) received $457M. The Department of Veterans Affairs is also obligating funds to Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $411M and to telecommunications and IT services such as Wired Telecommunications Carriers at $322M and Other Computer Related Services at $256M. Top vendors receiving FY2027 to date obligations include TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $900M, QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $247M, and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC at $226M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Veterans Affairs spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $5.6B on contracts in FY2027 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs recorded 11,569 awards with an average award size of $487K, and obligations are down 93.4% year over year. Prior fiscal years were larger in scale: $56.4B in FY22, $61.7B in FY23, $66.9B in FY24, $78.3B in FY25, and $85.8B in FY26."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Veterans Affairs buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs buys health and medical services, construction, telecommunications, and IT services in FY2027 to date. The Top 10 NAICS categories show Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $1.1B, Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) at $457M, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $411M, Wired Telecommunications Carriers at $322M, and Other Computer Related Services at $256M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Veterans Affairs's largest contractors?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs's largest contractors in FY2027 to date are TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $900M, QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $247M, and AT&T ENTERPRISES, LLC at $226M. Other top recipients include SIGNATURE CHOICE III, LLC at $226M, ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $151M, and THUNDERCAT TECHNOLOGY, LLC at $150M."
